  "query_preview": "..\nToday (Tuesday) through Thursday: A ridge of high pressure \nnudging in from the west will gain influence over the weather \npattern through the day today, ushering in drier air and\nclearing out cloud cover from west to east. Temperatures will\ntrend 5 to 10 degrees warmer than yesterday with highs today \nranging from the upper 60s in North ID to the low 80s in Central\nWA. Low temperatures tonight into Wednesday morning will fall \ninto the 40s to low 50s. Clear skies and light winds combined \nwith a moist boundary layer from recent rains may lead to fog \ndevelopment across the sheltered northern valleys.\n\nBy Wednesday and Thursday, temperatures will climb into the mid\nto upper 70s for North ID, upper 70s to low 80s for the eastern\nthird of WA, and low to upper 80s for Central WA. Winds will\nalso increase Wednesday and Thursday. For Wednesday, breezy \nconditions will be more localized across Central WA, primarily\naffecting the Waterville Plateau and Okanogan Valley. Winds on\nWednesday will be out of the south with sustained speeds in the\nteens and gusts up to 25 mph. \n\nOn Thursday, a trough pushes south into the region from over\nBritish Columbia and Alberta, tightening the pressure gradient\nacross the Inland Northwest and bringing widespread gusty\nwesterly winds. Sustained speeds Thursday will be in the 15 to 20\nmph range with gusts of 30 to 40 mph through the Cascade gaps,\nover the Waterville Plateau, across the Columbia Basin, and over\nthe Blue Mountains and Palouse. Blowing dust could be a \nconcern after having a break from precipitation Tuesday and\nWednesday. A weak disturbance ripples through the broader trough\nThursday evening, bringing a 30 to 60 percent chance of showers\nto the Cascades, ID Panhandle, and Blue Mountains.\n\nFriday through Monday: Heading into the weekend, a robust low \npressure system tracks down from the British Columbia coast and \nthen swings across the Inland Northwest, bringing back cool and \nshowery conditions along with more wind. Shower chances move \ninto the North Cascades Friday night into Saturday, then expand \nacross the region Saturday afternoon into Saturday night. Shower\nchances retreat to the mountains Sunday night into Monday as \nthe low pressure system moves out. Some moderate rains (0.25 to \n0.75 inches) are possible around the mountain zones Saturday and\nSunday, with lighter amounts (0.05 to 0.30 inches) elsewhere. \nEmbedded t-storms are also possible and may produce locally \nhigher precipitation amounts, though chances for t-storms look \nrelatively low (5 to 15%) at this point. \n\nTemperatures cool into the mid 60s to low 70s Friday through\nMonday with overnight lows in the 40s for most locations. Winds\nlook rather breezy out of the north-northeast Saturday\nafternoon, so that will be something to keep an eye on in the\ncoming days. /Fewkes",
